Opaque focus rim (dcsvr_two_layers_alpha=3, after 0024; native/quadviews/dcsvr_twolayer_ps.hlsl): 0024 sent the focus layer
with no blend flags and alpha 1 up to its edge. On the Pimax runtime that drew a fixed coloured line along the layer's
bottom edge, following the gaze (user, headset, 2026-11-22). The rig (artifacts/opaque-live) showed the submitted focus
layer clean on real and generated frames (bottom rows: no step above 3 codes), the undistorted compositor mirror clean,
and the distorted mirror one row with blue 0 and the next with red 255 along the focus rectangle's bottom edge: the
runtime's distortion pass reads past the edge of a layer that does not blend. Mode 0, whose alpha is 0 at the edges,
never showed it. Now mode 3 goes out with mode 0's flags (SOURCE_ALPHA | UNPREMULTIPLIED) and the focus pass writes alpha 1
except in a rim of 1.5 % of the rectangle per side: 0 in its outer half, rising to 1 across its inner half, the colour
there chosen so that the runtime's blend over the periphery layer gives the composed colour (the colour is the composed
one where alpha is 0, so a display path that ignores the alpha still shows the right image). The log line says so.
0, 1 and 2 are unchanged, byte for byte (tests/quadviews-composition, which now also checks the rim: every border texel
transparent, the blend in the ramp within 1 code of the composition).
